      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the area of the shaded region in the logo and compare it to the area of the entire square.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the radius of the circles\nEach circle is tangent to two sides of the square and its two adjacent circles. Therefore, the diagonal of one circle is equal to the side length of the square divided by 2 (since the circles are symmetrically placed).\n\nLet \\( r \\) be the radius of one circle. The diameter of one circle is \\( 2r \\), and this diameter is equal to the side length of the square divided by \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) (because the diagonal of the circle is equal to the side length of the square divided by \\(\\sqrt{2}\\)). Therefore, we have:\n\\[ 2r = \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{2}} = 10\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\\[ r = \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 5\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the area of one circle\nThe area \\( A \\) of one circle is given by the formula \\( A = \\pi r^2 \\):\n\\[ A = \\pi (5\\sqrt{2})^2 = \\pi \\times 50 = 50\\pi \\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the area of the square\nThe area of the entire square is:\n\\[ \\text{Area of the square} = 20 \\times 20 = 400 \\]\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the area of all four circles\nSince there are four circles, the total area of the four circles is:\n\\[ \\text{Total area of the circles} = 4 \\times 50\\pi = 200\\pi \\]\n\n### Step 5: Calculate the area of the shaded region\nThe shaded region is the area of the square minus the area of the four circles:\n\\[ \\text{Shaded area} = 400 - 200\\pi \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the shaded region is:\n\\[ \\boxed{400 - 200\\pi} \\]",
